# an ending
@title Dawn
@bg field
@mood hope

narrate The field gave up its best things and the Fever ended, and it did not end quietly.
narrate Word went out — not because anyone sent it, but because four thousand people walked home and told everyone they met.
narrate Corin did not want it. He spent the first three years telling people to go home and the next eight giving up.
bryn warm "I told you so."
corin warm "You did not."
bryn dry "I thought it very loudly."
narrate Cael's grave was at the edge of the field. Somebody planted something on it in the second year and it took.
narrate And Corin never remembered the bread.
narrate He read the entry back, every year, on the anniversary — a man with mud to his knees gave me half a loaf and told me to grow up strong — and every year it was a stranger's sentence in his own handwriting.
narrate And every year he believed it anyway, because there was a whole country that remembered it for him.
corin warm "I think I must have been happy. Everyone says so."

@ending dawn
